  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q63-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "63",
    "prompt": "The ratio of the coefficients of $x^6$ and $x^7$ in $(3+ax)^n$ is $1:2$. Similarly, the ratio of the coefficients of $x^7$ and $x^8$ is $2:3$.\n\nFind the value(s) of $a$ and $n$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p13-q63-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "a=6,\\quad n=13",
                "display": false
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p13-q63-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The consecutive-coefficient ratios give ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "a(n-6)=42",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " and ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "a(n-7)=36",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ".",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $c_r$ be the coefficient of $x^r$. From the general term,\n$$\\begin{align*}c_r&=\\binom{n}{r}3^{n-r}a^r\\\\\\frac{c_{r+1}}{c_r}&=\\frac{(n-r)a}{3(r+1)}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe given ratios therefore give\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{(n-6)a}{21}&=2\\\\a(n-6)&=42\\\\\\frac{(n-7)a}{24}&=\\frac32\\\\a(n-7)&=36.\\end{align*}$$\nSubtracting the two equations,\n$$\\begin{align*}a(n-6)-a(n-7)&=42-36\\\\a&=6\\\\6(n-7)&=36\\\\n&=13.\\end{align*}$$\nCheck:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{c_7}{c_6}&=\\frac{(13-6)6}{21}=2\\\\\\frac{c_8}{c_7}&=\\frac{(13-7)6}{24}=\\frac32.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $a=6$ and $n=13$ satisfy both ratios.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
